<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palawan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palawan is a long island in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> and the Sulu Sea, now a province. Its southern coasts were long linked to the sultanates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> and Sulu, which drew tribute from local chiefs, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> extended its control in the nineteenth century. Today it is known for its forests, coral reefs and the Puerto Princesa Underground River.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palawan.jpg" alt="Palawan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Theglennpalacio · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kayangan_Lake,_Coron_-_Palawan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Palawan is an archipelagic province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in the region of Mimaropa and the largest province in the country by area, at 14,649.73 square kilometres. Its islands stretch between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindoro">Mindoro</a> in the north-east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> in the south-west, lying between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> and the Sulu Sea. The province takes its name from its largest island, 450 kilometres long and 50 kilometres wide. It comprises roughly 1,780 islands and islets, including the Calamianes group in the north-east, Balabac Island off the southern tip and the Cuyo Islands in the Sulu Sea. Unlike most of the Philippines, Palawan belongs biogeographically to Sundaland, with fauna and flora related to those of Borneo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Researchers led by Robert Bradford Fox found evidence in the Tabon Caves that humans have lived on Palawan for more than 50,000 years, along with the bone fragments of an individual known as Tabon Man. The oldest inhabitants are the Palaw'an, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batak">Batak</a>, Tagbanwa and Tau't Bato of the interior and highlands and of the Calamianes Islands, who traditionally practise animist anito religions. Later groups settled the coasts. In the 1200s Palawan, Calamian and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> came under the nation of Sandao, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the more powerful Ma-i in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindoro">Mindoro</a>. The Molbog of the south, and the Cuyonon and Agutaynon, arrived later.</p><p>The northern Calamianes Islands were the first to come under Spanish authority. In the early 17th century Spanish friars sent missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a>, Agutaya, Taytay and Cagayancillo, but met resistance from Moro communities, and churches enclosed by garrisons were built against Moro raids. The Spanish census of 1818 recorded 4,486 native families and 29 Spanish-Filipino families on Palawan, then called Isla de Paragua. In 1902, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> established civil rule in northern Palawan as the province of Paragua. In 1905 the province was reorganised to include the south, renamed Palawan, and given Puerto Princesa as capital.</p><p>During the Japanese occupation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>, called Palawan's Fighting One Thousand, resisted, helped escaping American prisoners of war and guided the landings of the 8th Army. Palawan also felt the effect of martial law, declared by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a> in 1972. Later settlement from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> and elsewhere changed the island's population and languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Palawan has about 968,795 people, according to the 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The capital and largest city, Puerto Princesa, is administered independently of the province. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> is spoken by more than half the people, and the native Cuyonon and Palawano are also used. The economy is basically agricultural, with palay (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>), corn and coconut the main crops, along with fishing, logging and mining. The Puerto Princesa Subterranean River National Park, listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1999, and the Tubbataha Reef Marine Park, listed in 1993, are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palawan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sulu's reach extended through tributary ties to coastal chiefs in <b>Palawan</b>, Basilan and northern and eastern Borneo; according to Sulu tradition, the Sultan of Brunei granted it the lands of northern Borneo in return for help in Brunei's civil war of 1660–1673.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
